WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.302
Ivy has two accounts with a payments company, with 50.00 in each.

2
00:00:07.302 --> 00:00:13.956
The company lets one account go below zero, as long as the two together stay at zero or above.

3
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:24.605
One evening, in the same second, she pays 80.00 for groceries from one account, and her phone company takes 80.00 from the other.

4
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.336
Each payment checks her total, sees 100.00, and goes through.

5
00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:33.603
Ivy ends the evening at minus 60.00.

6
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.442
Each one checked first.

7
00:00:35.442 --> 00:00:37.117
So how did that happen?

8
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:38.501
Hold that question.

9
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.927
Picture two flatmates and a fridge with two cakes.

10
00:00:41.927 --> 00:00:45.555
The house rule: always leave at least one cake for guests.

11
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.416
At the same moment, both open the fridge.

12
00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:52.211
Each sees two cakes, so each thinks, fine, I can take one.

13
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:55.993
One takes the chocolate cake, the other takes the lemon one.

14
00:00:55.993 --> 00:00:59.317
They never reached for the same cake, so nobody argued.

15
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:01.432
And the guests get an empty fridge.

16
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.422
Let's map it.

17
00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:04.592
The cakes are Ivy's two accounts.

18
00:01:04.592 --> 00:01:08.013
The rule over both cakes is the rule over her total.

19
00:01:08.013 --> 00:01:13.604
And each flatmate is one payment, checking everything but changing only its own part.

20
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:16.095
Now inside the database.

21
00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:17.765
Ivy is customer 9.

22
00:01:17.765 --> 00:01:23.892
Her accounts, 109 and 209, hold 5,000 each, counted in hundredths.

23
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:28.226
Two withdrawals of 8,000 arrive together, one from each account.

24
00:01:28.226 --> 00:01:37.648
Each runs in its own transaction at Repeatable Read, so each reads from a snapshot, a picture of the committed data taken at its first statement.

25
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:41.744
Each adds up her balances and sees 10,000: allowed.

26
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:44.812
Transaction A takes 8,000 from 109.

27
00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:49.852
Its draft says minus 3,000, and it holds the lock on that row.

28
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:53.891
Transaction B takes 8,000 from 209.

29
00:01:53.891 --> 00:01:59.071
A's lock is on 109, not 209, so B doesn't wait.

30
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.581
Now both commit.

31
00:02:00.581 --> 00:02:02.263
Does either one fail?

32
00:02:02.263 --> 00:02:03.544
Pause and guess.

33
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:07.654
Neither.

34
00:02:07.654 --> 00:02:09.895
Both commits succeed.

35
00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:17.471
109 is minus 3,000, 209 is minus 3,000, and Ivy's total is minus 6,000.

36
00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:19.392
No error anywhere.

37
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:21.353
Well, the fridge is empty.

38
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:23.425
This is called write skew.

39
00:02:23.425 --> 00:02:30.259
Each transaction read rows that the other one then changed, and each write was right for the data it saw.

40
00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:34.640
Run one after the other, the second would have seen 2,000 and refused.

41
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.992
Run together, they made a state that no order of the two could have made.

42
00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:44.525
And Repeatable Read only complains when two transactions change the same row.

43
00:02:44.525 --> 00:02:45.708
These two didn't.

44
00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:56.585
Any rule that reads rows it doesn't write has this hole: a daily limit checked at several card readers, two bookings of one meeting room, or two refunds of one payment.

45
00:02:56.833 --> 00:02:58.884
The label won't save you either.

46
00:02:58.884 --> 00:03:06.255
Read Committed lets it through, and so does Oracle's level called SERIALIZABLE, which is really snapshot isolation.

47
00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:10.804
A sign on the fridge that says serializable doesn't keep a cake in it.

48
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.401
The first fix: lock the fridge door before you look.

49
00:03:14.401 --> 00:03:19.924
Each transaction first locks all of Ivy's accounts with SELECT FOR UPDATE, then checks.

50
00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.956
B's lock request waits until A commits.

51
00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:28.444
Then it reads the newest values, minus 3,000 and 5,000, and refuses.

52
00:03:28.444 --> 00:03:32.427
Withdrawals for one customer now run one at a time.

53
00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:35.530
The second fix: PostgreSQL's Serializable.

54
00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:40.915
It runs from snapshots as before, but it also notes what each transaction read.

55
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.090
A read 209, which B wrote, and B read 109, which A wrote.

56
00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:52.655
No order of the two explains that, so B's commit fails with SQLSTATE 40001.

57
00:03:52.867 --> 00:03:55.701
The program runs B again from the start.

58
00:03:55.701 --> 00:04:01.371
Its new snapshot includes A's commit: the total is 2,000, and the check refuses.

59
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:06.447
The catch: your code has to retry, and the retry has to be safe to repeat.

60
00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:13.487
So the moral of the story: checking first isn't enough when the check reads rows that someone else may change.

61
00:04:13.487 --> 00:04:16.496
Lock what you read, or let Serializable catch it.

62
00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:17.896
One quick check.

63
00:04:17.896 --> 00:04:25.803
Two refunds of one payment each check how much is left, then each writes its own refund row, at Repeatable Read.

64
00:04:25.803 --> 00:04:27.568
Can they refund too much?

65
00:04:27.568 --> 00:04:28.697
Pause and think.

66
00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:32.205
Yes.

67
00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:36.211
Each reads the same amount left and writes a different row.

68
00:04:36.211 --> 00:04:40.828
That's write skew again: two cakes, two flatmates, one empty fridge.
